- Functions and Business Rules:
  - Schedule Engine: parse cron expressions; calculate next run in given timezone; support UI-based schedule builder with equivalent cron expression output.
  - Trigger evaluation: handle time-based triggers, event-based triggers, and conditional triggers; enforce safety rails before running.
  - Input Template Rendering: render input payloads with variables; support default values and schema validation.
  - Permissions/Automation: enforce access controls per cronjob; support escalation to approvals when needed.
  - Safety Rails: ensure confirmations for sensitive actions; block execution until confirmations are met.
  - Retry and Dead-letter: implement exponential backoff, dead-letter routing, and retry counters per run.
  - Run Outputs: store logs, traces between agents, diffs, and artifacts; provide audit trails for each run.
- Validation and Integrity:
  - Validate cronjob shapes on create/update; guard against missing required fields.
  - Ensure all arrays are not null; default to empty arrays in responses as needed.
  - Normalize all API responses to expected shapes; robust error handling with meaningful messages.

Integration
- Data Flow:
  - Master Dashboard -> Cronjobs Dashboard: fetch aggregated status and quick actions.
  - Cronjob List -> Cronjob Detail: drill-down for editing/configuring.
  - Cronjob Detail -> Workflow Editor/Templates: select templates, versioned templates, and simulate runs.
  - Run history and outputs -> Approvals: surface any required approvals and SLA statuses.
  - Content Pipeline Module & Approvals: Cronjobs may trigger content pipelines with end-to-end artifacts.
- State and Caching:
  - Implement client-side caching for lists and templates; server-side caching for heavy computations like run histories.
  - Invalidate caches on mutations (create/update/trigger/run/approve).

User Experience Flow
1) User lands on Cronjobs Dashboard Master Page; sees a high-level Cronjobs panel, approvals queue, and system health.
2) User uses SearchBar and FiltersBar to locate cronjobs by target, status, or owner; results render instantly with safe guards for data shapes.
3) User clicks a Cronjob Card to open the Cronjob Detail editor; can view/edit schedule, triggers, input templates, constraints, safety rails, retry policy, and outputs.
4) User uses ScheduleBuilder to modify run cadence; preview next run timestamp; save changes.
5) User selects Run Now to trigger an ad-hoc run; monitor status in real-time or via run outputs.
6) If the cronjob requires approvals, the ApprovalsPanel surfaces the item; user submits approvals with comments to satisfy SLA constraints.
7) User opens Create Cronjob to launch a builder with guided steps: select target, choose a workflow/template, configure schedule, input templates, constraints, safety rails, and auto-approval constraints.
8) From a Cronjob Detail, user can navigate to the connected Workflow Template Library to attach or simulate templates, or to the Content Pipeline module to observe end-to-end content automation implications.
9) Data visualizations show run histories, traces, and performance metrics in charts consistent with the visual style.

Technical Specifications

Data Models: Schema details (illustrative)
- Cronjob
  - id: UUID
  - name: string
  - enabled: boolean
  - paused: boolean
  - schedule: JSON (or string cron) 
  - timezone: string
  - targetType: 'agent' | 'template'
  - targetId: UUID
  - inputTemplate: JSON
  - permissions: string
  - automationLevel: string
  - constraints: JSON
  - safetyRails: JSON
  - retryPolicy: JSON
  - outputs: JSON
  - ownerId: UUID
  - createdAt: timestamp
  - updatedAt: timestamp
- CronjobRun
  - id: UUID
  - cronjobId: UUID
  - startedAt: timestamp
  - finishedAt: timestamp
  - status: string
  - outcome: JSON
- Approval
  - id: UUID
  - cronjobId: UUID
  - approvers: UUID[]
  - status: 'open'|'approved'|'rejected'
  - SLAStart: timestamp
  - SLAEnd: timestamp
  - comments: JSON
  - createdAt: timestamp
  - updatedAt: timestamp
- Template
  - id: UUID
  - name: string
  - version: number
  - content: JSON
  - type: 'cronjob' | 'workflow'
  - createdAt: timestamp
  - updatedAt: timestamp

API Endpoints (Routes and methods)
- GET /cronjobs
  - Retrieve list with pagination, search, and filters
  - Response: { data: Cronjob[], total: number } or { data: [], total: 0 }
- GET /cronjobs/:id
  - Retrieve a cronjob detail
  - Response: Cronjob object
- POST /cronjobs
  - Create a new cronjob
  - Body: CronjobCreateInput (validated)
- PUT /cronjobs/:id
  - Update cronjob properties (schedule, triggers, constraints, etc.)
- POST /cronjobs/:id/trigger
  - Run now for the cronjob
- GET /cronjobs/:id/runs
  - Retrieve run history for a cronjob
- GET /cronjobs/:id/outputs/:runId
  - Retrieve outputs/logs/traces for a specific run
- GET /approvals
  - List approvals queue
- POST /approvals/:id/approve
  - Approve action with optional comments
- POST /approvals/:id/reject
  - Reject action with optional comments
- GET /templates
  - Retrieve templates library (cronjob/workflow templates)
- GET /templates/:id
  - Retrieve specific template details
- POST /templates/:id/simulate
  - Simulate a run against a template
- GET /health
  - System health indicators and metrics

Security
- Authentication: OAuth2 or session-based; all endpoints protected.
- Authorization: Role-based access control (RBAC) with per-cronjob permissions; enforcement on backend for create/update/run/actions.
- Data integrity: Input validation with schema validation (JSON Schema or TS validators). All mutations require proper permission checks.
- Audit: All mutations produce audit logs with actor, timestamp, and before/after states; run artifacts are stored for traceability and replay.

Validation and Validation Rules
- Validation on all inputs:
  - Required fields must be present; string lengths validated.
  - schedule must be valid (cron or UI schedule object) with timezone awareness.
  - inputTemplate must include required keys; variables must be defined or have defaults.
  - constraints, safetyRails, retryPolicy must be valid JSON structures conforming to predefined schemas.
- Runtime safety:
  - All array operations guarded: (array ?? []).map(...)
  - All API responses checked: Array.isArray(response?.data) ? response.data : []
  - State initialization with correct types: useState<YourType[]>([])
  - Optional chaining used for nested API values: api?.payload?.nested

Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] The Cronjobs Dashboard loads with no runtime errors and displays a list of cronjobs, each with next run, last run, status, and lightweight controls.
- [ ] Creating a new cronjob via the builder results in a persisted cronjob with correct schedule, target, and defaults for safety rails and retry policy; UI reflects the new item and it appears in the list.
- [ ] Schedule Builder must support both cron expressions and UI-based scheduling with correct timezone handling and a valid next-run preview.
- [ ] Run Now action triggers a cronjob, and the run is visible in the run history with status updates (pending/running/completed) and logs.
- [ ] Approvals workflow is functional: open approvals appear in the queue, can be approved/rejected with comments, and SLA timers reflect status.
- [ ] Cronjob Detail editor correctly saves updates; validations fail with helpful messages for invalid configurations.
- [ ] Performance: initial load time under 2 seconds for the cronjob list on a typical dataset; per-run history loads in under 300ms after initial pagination.
- [ ] All data accesses are null-safe and guarded as per runtime safety rules.

UI/UX Guidelines
- Align with the project’s design system and visual style: dark theme, flat color blocks, strong typography, card-based layouts, and accessible contrast.
- Use consistent visual language for actions: primary blue (#4F8CFF), success green, warning yellow, and error red.
- Provide clear affordances for per-cronjob actions and bulk actions if applicable.
- Ensure keyboard accessibility: focus states, tab ordering, and ARIA attributes for dynamic controls.
- Responsive behavior: grid-based layout that adapts from 12-column desktop to stacked mobile views without compromising readability or accessibility.

Visual Style

Color Palette and Typography
- Follow the specified palette and typography tokens. No gradients beyond solid color blocks. Use the specified color codes for consistency across charts, badges, and callouts.

Data Visualization
- Where charts are used (e.g., run success rates, next run timelines, or SLA progress), prefer line charts with neon accent lines and muted gridlines on dark backgrounds. Provide accessible legends and tooltips.

Implementation Notes and Best Practices
- Always guard against null/undefined values before array operations; use data ?? [] and Array.isArray checks.
- Use useState<T Cronjob[]>([]) for cronjob lists; ensure all array state is initialized as an empty array.
- Centralize data validation logic on the backend; return stable shapes to the frontend.
- Provide thorough error handling and user-friendly error messages across all UI paths.
- Favor optimistic UI updates where safe (e.g., enabling/disabling a cronjob) with rollback if server returns an error.
- Include unit tests and integration tests for critical data flows (cronjob CRUD, schedule parse/validation, run triggers, approvals).

Deliverables
- Fully-structured frontend components and pages described above, wired to backend APIs or mock services with the same shapes.
- Backend data models and endpoints as described, with robust validation and security guards.
- Documentation: API contracts, data models, UI interaction patterns, and testing strategy.
- CI/CD considerations: linting, type checks, tests, and deployment notes for staging and production environments.

Notes
- Prioritize runtime safety and null-safety in all code.
- Maintain alignment with LifeOps’ multi-agent orchestration philosophy: explainable actions, traceable runs, and reversible operations with audit trails.
- If any assumption is needed, document it clearly in the prompt or code comments and implement safe defaults.

This prompt provides a comprehensive blueprint for building the Cronjobs Dashboard and its connected systems within the LifeOps ecosystem, ensuring robust functionality, maintainable architecture, and a consistent user experience.

